> Can you suggest some multiple choice questions to check that they've done the reading?
**assistant**:
> ### Multiple-choice questions
> 
> 1. **How does the article distinguish mitigation from adaptation?**  
>    A. Mitigation reduces climate impacts; adaptation reduces emissions.  
>    B. Mitigation reduces greenhouse-gas emissions; adaptation adjusts to climate effects.  
>    C. Mitigation is private action; adaptation is public action.  
>    D. Mitigation is long-term; adaptation is necessarily short-term.  
> 
> 2. **According to the IPCC definition cited, resilience is the ability to:**  
>    A. eliminate all climate risks.  
>    B. predict climate change precisely.  
>    C. anticipate, absorb, accommodate, or recover from hazardous events.  
>    D. reduce greenhouse-gas concentrations.  
> 
> 3. **Which is *not* one of the four main analytical tools emphasized in the review?**  
>    A. Integrated assessment models  
>    B. Econometric analysis  
>    C. Economy-wide simulation models  
>    D. Randomized controlled trials  
> 
> 4. **In early integrated assessment models, adaptation was generally:**  
>    A. assumed to be optimally embedded in the damage function.  
>    B. excluded because it has no economic effects.  
>    C. represented only through migration decisions.  
>    D. modeled as identical to mitigation.  
> 
> 5. **What is a central limitation of cross-sectional (“Ricardian”) studies for adaptation?**  
>    A. They cannot observe differences across climates.  
>    B. They reveal long-run outcomes but not adjustment processes or adaptation costs.  
>    C. Weather is endogenous in cross-sectional data.  
>    D. They only apply to industrialized countries.  
> 
> 6. **Why can time-series studies be useful for identifying weather impacts?**  
>    A. Weather variations are generally exogenous and spatial institutions are held constant.  
>    B. They fully capture long-run climate adaptation.  
>    C. They eliminate all uncertainty about future climate.  
>    D. They do not require data.  
> 
> 7. **Which sector has the richest empirical evidence on private adaptation?**  
>    A. Banking  
>    B. Agriculture  
>    C. Manufacturing  
>    D. Telecommunications  
> 
> 8. **The article argues that “autonomous adaptation” is a misleading term because adaptation:**  
>    A. is always funded by governments.  
>    B. only occurs after disasters.  
>    C. results from deliberate decisions shaped by information, incentives, and policy.  
>    D. is impossible without international climate finance.  
> 
> 9. **Which is one of the three major public-policy roles in adaptation?**  
>    A. Replacing all private adaptation decisions  
>    B. Providing climate-resilient public goods  
>    C. Setting a universal global adaptation price  
>    D. Eliminating uncertainty about future climate  
> 
> 10. **An adaptation investment should be brought forward when:**  
>     A. delay locks in vulnerability that is costly to reverse.  
>     B. its benefits occur only in the distant future.  
>     C. uncertainty is completely eliminated.  
>     D. mitigation has already failed.  
> 
> 11. **How does economic development generally affect adaptation in the article’s framework?**  
>     A. It lowers both demand and supply.  
>     B. It improves adaptive capacity and increases demand for climate protection.  
>     C. It eliminates exposure and vulnerability.  
>     D. It affects mitigation but not adaptation.  
> 
> 12. **In strict economic terms, adaptation and mitigation are generally treated as:**  
>     A. substitutes, although an effective climate response requires both.  
>     B. unrelated policy domains.  
>     C. perfect complements in every circumstance.  
>     D. alternative names for the same activity.  
> 
> ### Answer key
> 1. B · 2. C · 3. D · 4. A · 5. B · 6. A · 7. B · 8. C · 9. B · 10. A · 11. B · 12. A
